---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Related from '../components/Related.astro';
import { RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE, SOURCES } from '../lib/editorial.js';

const title = 'Methodology: Calculator Math, Sources & Research Standards';
const description =
  'How Restaurant Tip Calculator handles percentages, tax, fees, splitting and rounding, plus how tipping data and etiquette sources are selected and reviewed.';

const nav = [
  { href: '#calculator-method', label: 'Calculator math' },
  { href: '#research-method', label: 'Research' },
  { href: '#sources', label: 'Sources' },
];

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  '@id': 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/methodology/#breadcrumb',
  itemListElement: [
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 1,
      name: 'Restaurant Tip Calculator',
      item: 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 2,
      name: 'Methodology',
      item: 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/methodology/',
    },
  ],
});
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/methodology/" jsonLd={jsonLd} nav={nav}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="guide-intro"><a href="/">Restaurant tip calculator</a> / Methodology</p>
    <h1>Methodology: How the Calculator and Research Work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This page explains what the calculator actually computes, how cents are rounded and split, and how the site's
      research separates measured data from survey answers and etiquette recommendations.
    </p>
    <p class="menu-src">Research last reviewed: {RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE}.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="calculator-method">
    <h2>How the calculator computes your bill</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Bill, tax, and fees</h3>
      <p>
        The bill amount is the default basis for a percentage tip. Tax can be entered separately. If
        <strong>Include tax in percentage tip basis</strong> is off, the tip percentage applies only to the bill
        amount; if it is on, the percentage applies to bill plus entered tax. Other fees and delivery fees are added
        to the payable total but are not included in the percentage tip basis.
      </p>

      <h3>Percentage tips</h3>
      <p>
        Percentage input is stored to two decimal places of percentage precision. The calculator multiplies the tip
        basis by the selected percentage and rounds the resulting tip to the nearest cent using deterministic
        half-up rounding.
      </p>

      <h3>Custom dollar tips</h3>
      <p>
        A custom dollar tip bypasses percentage calculation. The exact entered tip amount, in cents, is added to the
        bill along with any tax and fees.
      </p>

      <h3>Payable total</h3>
      <p>
        The total shown is the sum of the entered bill, entered tax, entered other fees, any entered delivery fee, and
        the calculated or custom tip. The calculator can only know amounts you enter; it does not claim to know a
        restaurant's final card charge or any unentered surcharge.
      </p>
    </div>

    <table class="worked">
      <caption>Example: $60 bill, $4.80 tax, 20% tip</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Tip basis choice</th><th scope="col">Tip</th><th scope="col">Total with entered tax</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tax excluded from tip basis</td><td>$12.00</td><td>$76.80</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tax included in tip basis</td><td>$12.96</td><td>$77.76</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="splitting">
    <h2>How exact splitting and round-up differ</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Exact cent-reconciled split</h3>
      <p>
        The calculator first divides the payable total into whole cents. When the cents do not divide evenly, the
        remainder is distributed as one additional cent to the required number of shares. The displayed shares always
        add back to the exact payable total.
      </p>
      <p>
        Example: $10.00 split three ways becomes one share of $3.34 and two shares of $3.33. Those shares total exactly
        $10.00.
      </p>

      <h3>Whole-dollar round-up</h3>
      <p>
        Round-up is intentionally different. It raises each person's share to the next whole dollar. That can collect
        more than the exact payable total, so the calculator shows both the amount collected and the extra amount
        created by rounding. It does not silently treat that extra money as part of the restaurant bill.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="research-method">
    <h2>How tipping research is selected and described</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Source hierarchy</h3>
      <p>
        For laws, wage rules, and federal tax treatment, the site prefers primary government sources such as the U.S.
        Department of Labor and Internal Revenue Service. For population or transaction statistics, it prefers the
        original research organization or platform that produced the dataset. Etiquette recommendations are sourced
        to established etiquette guidance and are labeled as recommendations.
      </p>

      <h3>Three evidence types are kept separate</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Observed behavior</strong> is what a transaction dataset records. <strong>Stated behavior</strong> is
        what survey respondents say they do or would do. <strong>Etiquette guidance</strong> is advice about what
        someone should or may choose to do. A recommendation is not presented as an average, and a survey answer is not
        presented as an observed transaction.
      </p>

      <h3>Freshness</h3>
      <p>
        Research-heavy pages show a real review date rather than automatically displaying today's date. During a
        review, the site checks the publication date, underlying data period, methodology, and whether a newer primary
        source is available. An old survey does not become "2026 data" merely because this site reviewed it in 2026.
      </p>

      <h3>Limits and local variation</h3>
      <p>
        Restaurant policies, platform compensation rules, state wage laws, and local consumer rules can vary and
        change. The site avoids turning a national calculator into individualized legal advice and does not infer who
        receives a restaurant fee solely from its label or percentage.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="sources">
    <h2>Core research sources used in the September 2026 review</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.toastTipping}>Toast — Restaurant Tipping Trends</a>
        — Q1 2026 platform transaction data; card and digital tips, with cash excluded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.pewTipping}>Pew Research Center — Services Americans do and don't tip for, and how much</a>
        — November 2023 survey research.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.dolFactSheet15}>U.S. Department of Labor — Fact Sheet #15</a>
        — federal tipped-employee and tip-credit framework.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.dolTippedWages}>U.S. Department of Labor — Minimum Wages for Tipped Employees</a>
        — state-by-state tipped wage rules.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.irsTips}>Internal Revenue Service — Tip recordkeeping and reporting</a>
        — federal tax distinction between voluntary tips and mandatory service charges.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.emilyPost}>Emily Post — General Tipping Guide</a>
        — etiquette recommendations for sit-down service, buffet service, delivery and other contexts.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related />
</Layout>
